{"surah": 99, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "إِذَا زُلْزِلَتِ ٱلْأَرْضُ زِلْزَالَهَا ", "translation": "When the earth is shaken with its [final] earthquake", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "زُلْزِلَتِ", "translit": "ِtَlِzْlُz", "root": "زلزل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "زِلْزَالَهَا", "translit": "āَhَlāَzْlِz", "root": "زلزل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "وَأَخْرَجَتِ ٱلْأَرْضُ أَثْقَالَهَا ", "translation": "And the earth discharges its burdens[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَخْرَجَتِ", "translit": "ِtَjgَrْhkَaʾَw", "root": "خرج"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَثْقَالَهَا", "translit": "āَhَlāَqْhtَaʾ", "root": "ثقل"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَقَالَ ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ مَا لَهَا ", "translation": "And man says,[1] \"What is [wrong] with it?\" -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْإِنسَٰنُ", "translit": "ُnَٰsnِiʾْlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "يَوْمَئِذٍ تُحَدِّثُ أَخْبَارَهَا ", "translation": "That Day, it will report its news", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تُحَدِّثُ", "translit": "ُhtِّdَḥُt", "root": "حدث"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَخْبَارَهَا", "translit": "āَhَrāَbْhkَaʾ", "root": "خبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "بِأَنَّ رَبَّكَ أَوْحَىٰ لَهَا ", "translation": "Because your Lord has inspired [i.e., commanded] it.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بِأَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَبَّكَ", "translit": "َkَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَوْحَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَḥْwَaʾ", "root": "وحي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَهَا", "translit": "āَhَl", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "يَوْمَئِذٍ يَصْدُرُ ٱلنَّاسُ أَشْتَاتًا لِّيُرَوْا۟ أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ ", "translation": "That Day, the people will depart[1] separated [into categories] to be shown [the result of] their deeds.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَئِذٍ", "translit": "ٍhdِyʾَmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَصْدُرُ", "translit": "ُrُdْṣَy", "root": "صدر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاسُ", "translit": "ُsāَّnlٱ", "root": "انس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أَشْتَاتًا", "translit": "āًtāَtْhsَaʾ", "root": "شتت"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِّيُرَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَrُyِّl", "root": "راي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَعْمَٰلَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَlَٰmْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عمل"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "فَمَن يَعْمَلْ مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍ خَيْرًا يَرَهُۥ ", "translation": "So whoever does an atom's weight[1] of good will see it,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَمَن", "translit": "nَmَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَعْمَلْ", "translit": "ْlَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِثْقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqْhtِm", "root": "ثقل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ذَرَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّrَhd", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "خَيْرًا", "translit": "āًrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrَy", "root": "راي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَمَن يَعْمَلْ مِثْقَالَ ذَرَّةٍ شَرًّا يَرَهُۥ ", "translation": "And whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَن", "translit": "nَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَعْمَلْ", "translit": "ْlَmْʿعَy", "root": "عمل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِثْقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqْhtِm", "root": "ثقل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ذَرَّةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَّrَhd", "root": "ذرر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "شَرًّا", "translit": "āًّrَhs", "root": "شرر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَرَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَrَy", "root": "راي"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"6": [{"arabic": "الله ونسوه والله على كل شيء شهيد} وقال تعالى {يومئذ يصدر الناس أشتاتا ليروا أعمالهم فمن يعمل مثقال ذرة خيرا يره ومن يعمل مثقال ذرة شرا يره} وقال تعالى {ثم توفى كل نفس ما كسبت وهم لا يظلمون} وقال تعالى {يوم تجد كل نفس ما عملت من خير محضرا وما عملت من سوء تود لو أن بينها وبينه أمدا بعيدا ويحذركم الله نفسه} وقال تعالى {واعلموا أن الله يعلم ما في أنفسكم فاحذروه} فعرف أرباب البصائر من جملة العباد أن الله تعالى لهم بالمرصاد وأنهم سيناقشون في الحساب ويطالبون بمثاقيل الذر من الخطرات واللحظات وتحققوا أنه لا ينجيهم من هذه", "english": "To proceed: God Most High has said: {And We shall set up the scales of justice for the Day of Resurrection, so no soul shall be wronged in anything; and if it be the weight of a grain of mustard seed, We shall bring it forth, and sufficient are We as Reckoners} (21:47). And He said: {And the Book shall be placed, and you will see the criminals fearful of what is in it, and they will say, \"Woe to us! What is this book that leaves neither small nor great but has enumerated it?\" And they will find what they did present, and your Lord wrongs no one} (18:49). And He said: {On the Day when God will raise them all together and inform them of what they did — God has enumerated it, and they have forgotten it, and God is Witness over all things} (58:6). And He said: {On that Day, people will issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds. So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it} (99:6-8). And He said: {Then every soul shall be paid in full for what it has earned, and they shall not be wronged} (2:281). And He said: {On the Day when every soul will find what it did of good present, and what it did of evil, it will wish that between it and that there were a far distance; and God warns you of Himself} (3:30). And He said: {And know that God knows what is in your souls, so beware of Him} (2:235).", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1230", "section_title_en": "Exposition of the Reality of Truthfulness (Al-Sidq), Its Meaning, and Its Grades — Book of Vigilance and Self-Reckoning, Being the Eighth Book of the Quarter of Deliverance from the Revival of the Religious Sciences", "section_index": 1230, "anchors": "- Root: رقب (Raqaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To watch, guard, observe; a watcher (raqīb) is one who stands vigilant over something, awaiting its occurrence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Raqaba denotes protective watchfulness; al-raqīb is a divine attribute signifying His perpetual oversight of all that transpires, with no moment of neglect.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): physical watching/awaiting. Metaphorical (majāz): \"This matter is upon your necks (fī riqābikum)\" — i.e., an obligation you must vigilantly uphold; \"the death is in the necks\" — imminent and inescapable.\n- Root: حسب (Hasaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To count, reckon, compute; al-ḥisāb is the act of enumeration and settlement of accounts.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-ḥisāb is the employment of number; divine reckoning (ḥisāb) on the Day of Resurrection is the exacting appraisal of deeds, even to the atom's weight.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: counting wealth or deeds. Metaphorical: \"they reckon news (yaḥtasibūna al-akhbār)\" — they investigate and ascertain, as one places knowledge in place of conjecture.\n- Root: قلب (Qalaba)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, transform; the heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning of states.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Qalaba is the turning of a thing from one state to another; the heart's inner thoughts (khaṭarāt) are its successive movements, which God surveys.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: turning a garment or object. Metaphorical: \"the teacher turned the boys\" (sent them home); \"the merchant turned the goods\" (scrutinized them) — thus, inner reflection (taqallub) is the soul's scrutiny of its own motives.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — The particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, as in \"إنما يحاسبهم لتعلم كل نفس\" — the sole purpose of reckoning is specified, excluding all other ends.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "7": [{"arabic": "درجاتهم وبالجملة {فمن يعمل مثقال ذرة خيرا يره ومن يعمل مثقال ذرة شرا يره} ومن قصد الله تعالى بالعلم أي علم كان نفعه ورفعه لا محالة الوظيفة العاشرة أن يعلم نسبة العلوم إلى المقصد كيما يؤثر الرفيع القريب على البعيد والمهم على غيره ومعنى المهم ما يهمك ولا يهمك إلا شأنك في الدنيا والآخرة وإذا لم يمكنك الجمع بين ملاذ الدنيا ونعيم الآخرة كما نطق به القرآن وشهد له من نور البصائر ما يجري مجرى العيان فالأهم ما يبقى أبد الآباد وعند ذلك تصير الدنيا منزلا والبدن مركبا والأعمال سعيا إلى المقصد ولا مقصد إلا لقاء الله تعالى ففيه النع", "english": "By this I do not mean the belief which the common man picks up by inheritance or by rote acquisition, nor the method of theological disputation and argumentation for fortifying one's creed against the evasions of opponents—as is the ultimate aim of the scholastic theologian (mutakallim). Rather, it is a kind of certainty which is the fruit of a light that God—may He be exalted—casts into the heart of a servant whose inward being has been purified by spiritual striving (mujāhadah) from all impurities, until he attains to the rank of the faith of Abū Bakr—may God be pleased with him—whose faith, were it weighed against the faith of all created beings, would outweigh them, as the Master of mankind—peace and blessings be upon him—bore witness for him. For in my view, what the common man believes and what the theologian arranges—who exceeds the common man only in the craft of speech (kalām), and for that very reason his craft is named \"kalām\"—is not that rank. Indeed, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, and the rest of the Companions—may God be pleased with them all—fell short of it, until Abū Bakr surpassed them by that secret which was deposited in his breast.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 43", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 43: On the Ordering of the Sciences and the Duties of the Seeker", "section_index": 43, "anchors": "- Root: ذوق (dh-w-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perceiving by taste; experiential apprehension\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Direct, unmediated perception—contrasted with mere cognition or hearsay\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal tasting vs. metaphorical \"savoring\" of knowledge\n- Root: سلك (s-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Entering into a path; threading through\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The spiritual wayfaring (sulūk) toward God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal road-taking vs. metaphorical spiritual journeying\n- Root: كشف (k-sh-f) — AVOIDED per constraints; substituted with Root: غور (gh-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Depth, profundity; the deep bed of a sea\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unfathomable depth of divine knowledge\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal deep water vs. metaphorical inaccessibility of ultimate truth\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — Governing the periodic suspension of meaning across clauses for rhetorical emphasis", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "8": [{"arabic": "درجاتهم وبالجملة {فمن يعمل مثقال ذرة خيرا يره ومن يعمل مثقال ذرة شرا يره} ومن قصد الله تعالى بالعلم أي علم كان نفعه ورفعه لا محالة الوظيفة العاشرة أن يعلم نسبة العلوم إلى المقصد كيما يؤثر الرفيع القريب على البعيد والمهم على غيره ومعنى المهم ما يهمك ولا يهمك إلا شأنك في الدنيا والآخرة وإذا لم يمكنك الجمع بين ملاذ الدنيا ونعيم الآخرة كما نطق به القرآن وشهد له من نور البصائر ما يجري مجرى العيان فالأهم ما يبقى أبد الآباد وعند ذلك تصير الدنيا منزلا والبدن مركبا والأعمال سعيا إلى المقصد ولا مقصد إلا لقاء الله تعالى", "english": "By this I do not mean the belief which the common man picks up by inheritance or by rote acquisition, nor the method of theological disputation and argumentation for fortifying one's creed against the evasions of opponents—as is the ultimate aim of the scholastic theologian (mutakallim). Rather, it is a kind of certainty which is the fruit of a light that God—may He be exalted—casts into the heart of a servant whose inward being has been purified by spiritual striving (mujāhadah) from all impurities, until he attains to the rank of the faith of Abū Bakr—may God be pleased with him—whose faith, were it weighed against the faith of all created beings, would outweigh them, as the Master of mankind—peace and blessings be upon him—bore witness for him. For in my view, what the common man believes and what the theologian arranges—who exceeds the common man only in the craft of speech (kalām), and for that very reason his craft is named \"kalām\"—is not that rank. Indeed, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, and the rest of the Companions—may God be pleased with them all—fell short of it, until Abū Bakr surpassed them by that secret which was deposited in his breast.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 43", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 43: On the Ordering of the Sciences and the Duties of the Seeker", "section_index": 43, "anchors": "- Root: ذوق (dh-w-q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Perceiving by taste; experiential apprehension\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Direct, unmediated perception—contrasted with mere cognition or hearsay\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal tasting vs. metaphorical \"savoring\" of knowledge\n- Root: سلك (s-l-k)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Entering into a path; threading through\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The spiritual wayfaring (sulūk) toward God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal road-taking vs. metaphorical spiritual journeying\n- Root: كشف (k-sh-f) — AVOIDED per constraints; substituted with Root: غور (gh-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Depth, profundity; the deep bed of a sea\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The unfathomable depth of divine knowledge\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal deep water vs. metaphorical inaccessibility of ultimate truth\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — Governing the periodic suspension of meaning across clauses for rhetorical emphasis", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"abdulbasit": {"1": [[1, 350, 1119], [2, 1119, 2105], [3, 2105, 3052], [4, 3052, 4651]], "2": [[1, 350, 1605], [2, 1605, 2491], [3, 2491, 4077]], "3": [[1, 350, 1602], [2, 1602, 3081], [3, 3081, 3695], [4, 3695, 4521]], "4": [[1, 350, 1888], [2, 1888, 3078], [3, 3078, 5096]], "5": [[1, 350, 1415], [2, 1415, 2274], [3, 2274, 3694], [4, 3694, 4495]], "6": [[1, 400, 1687], [2, 1687, 2652], [3, 2652, 3949], [4, 3949, 5477], [5, 5477, 6955], [6, 6955, 8344]], "7": [[1, 350, 1140], [2, 1140, 2378], [3, 2378, 3932], [4, 3932, 4893], [5, 4893, 6263], [6, 6263, 7159]], "8": [[1, 400, 1350], [2, 1350, 2626], [3, 2626, 4228], [4, 4228, 5219], [5, 5219, 6454], [6, 6454, 7377]]}, "minshawi": {"1": [[1, 420, 1419], [2, 1419, 2418], [3, 2418, 3377], [4, 3377, 5830]], "2": [[1, 280, 1810], [2, 1810, 2757], [3, 2757, 5280]], "3": [[1, 10, 1657], [2, 1657, 3130], [3, 3130, 3965], [4, 3965, 5450]], "4": [[1, 20, 1851], [2, 1851, 3076], [3, 3076, 6200]], "5": [[1, 10, 1186], [2, 1186, 2188], [3, 2188, 4000], [4, 4000, 5760]], "6": [[1, 340, 1915], [2, 1915, 3117], [3, 3117, 4681], [4, 4681, 6767], [5, 6767, 8778], [6, 8778, 10300]], "7": [[1, 540, 1355], [2, 1355, 2889], [3, 2889, 4790], [4, 4790, 5781], [5, 5781, 7532], [6, 7532, 8700]], "8": [[1, 510, 1712], [2, 1712, 3255], [3, 3255, 5167], [4, 5167, 616